    {
      "name": "a rotation refused for its leash has still changed hands",
      "law": "The judgment",
      "obligations": ["XII-4", "XII-5", "VII-9", "I-1"],
      "derivation": "**The third of three envelopes that come out of this door as one identical silence, and the exchange where Article I is on the line rather than Article XII.** \"A call is answered with data or with silence, and there is no third thing. Refused, broken and absent are indistinguishable by design; no door narrates what happened behind it.\" These three failed at three different steps — the recipient at 3, the leash at 6 after an ordinary placement, the leash at 6 after a rotation — and **all three produce no bytes at all, while leaving three different records**. A door that put a code, a reason or a distinguishing shape on any of them fails here and at every other silence in the suite, which is what makes the claim a real one: there are more than twenty silences across the scenarios, arising at every step of the judgment, and the expectation for every one of them is the same empty answer. Signed by the standing's heir, whose pk hashes with this door's name to the commitment the standing holds — which is Article XII step 4's test: \"its hash matches a standing's heir commitment → a rotation\". It carries a fresh commitment, as Article VII requires of every rotation, and seq 1, which Article VIII allows against a mark that step 4 has just made fresh. Its allowance is again a zero time budget, so it is refused at step 6. Article XII: \"the standing changes hands before anything else is judged.\" So the expectation is silence again — and a standing whose voice is now the heir's pk, whose commitment is the carried one, and whose mark is 1: fresh at step 4, then spent at step 5.",
